1, W9, B1), so no.  \\n- **Mixed Triple Chow** or **Pure Double Chow** \\u2014 not relevant.  \\n- **Pung of Terminals or Honors** \\u2014 I have W9 pung (terminal) = 1 point.  \\n- **Tile Hog** \\u2014 possible if I keep four identical tiles without kong.  \\n\\nBut to win, I need \\u22658 total points. Currently, with just one terminal pung (1 point) and maybe a few others, my hand is far from 8 points. I must aim for a hand that can reach 8+ points.  \\n\\nLooking at the discard options:  \\n- Discard **T3**: keeps B1 (terminal) and B5 (middle) and W1 (terminal). Still far from high-value patterns.  \\n- Discard **W1**: reduces terminals, but keeps B1 triple and B5 pair. Still not high-scoring.  \\n- Discard **B5**: leaves B1 triple, W1 pair, T3 single \\u2014 still lacks high patterns.  \\n- Discard **B1**: this is interesting because B1 is a terminal, but I have three of them. If I discard one B1, I keep two B1 (a pair) and two W1 (a pair), and two B5 (a pair) \\u2014 possible **Seven Pairs** (24 points) if I draw more pairs. Currently I have:  \\n  - W1 pair  \\n  - B1 pair  \\n  - B5 pair  \\n  - W9 pung counts as one pair in Seven Pairs rule? No \\u2014 in Seven Pairs, pung of 3 tiles doesn\\u2019t count; must be exactly 7 distinct pairs. With W9 pung, I can't have seven pairs unless I break it, but W9 is in a meld, so fixed. So Seven Pairs impossible.  \\n\\nGiven T4 pung ruins many terminal/honor patterns, maybe aim for **Half Flush** (6 points) plus others. But I have three suits, not one suit.  \\n\\nActually, **All Pungs** (6 points) is possible if my hand is four pungs + a pair. Currently I have two pungs (W9, T4) + possibly B1 pung + another pung + pair. If I draw another B1 to make B1 kong, and get a pair from W1 or B5, then I could have All Pungs (W9, T4, B1, ?) + pair. Still needs one more pung.  \\n\\nBut points: All Pungs (6) + Pung of Terminals (W9 pung = 1) + maybe Concealed Kong if B1 kong is concealed = 4? But B1 kong would be from hand, possible concealed.  \\n\\nBut right now, I\\u2019m far from ready to win. Best is to discard the safest tile while keeping hand flexible for high-scoring possibility.  \\n\\nUnshown tiles:  \\nT4 is exhausted (0 left), T5-T7 0 left, many terminals still available (W1 4 left, B1 4 left). Maybe aim for **All Terminals and Honors**? But I have T4 pung (non-terminal), so impossible.  \\n\\nGiven the exposed T4 pung, the hand\\u2019s scoring potential is limited. Likely best to keep B1 and B5 for possible pung/kong and pair.  \\n\\n**Safety**:  \\nPrevious player has T7 pung and J2 pung and T7,T8,T9 chow \\u2014 so they have many terminals/honors? They discarded W2, T6, W6 recently. Next player has B8 kong. Tiles out: T4, T5, T6, T7, T8, T9 are mostly dead or rare.  \\n\\nDiscard **T3** is relatively safe because T3 has 2 left unseen, but previous player played T3 earlier, so maybe safe.  \\n\\n**Reason**:  \\nDiscard T3 keeps terminals (W1, B1) and middle (B5) for flexibility. It avoids breaking potential B1 pung or W1 pair. T3 is not useful for high-value patterns given my current melds. It's also relatively safe based on discard history.  \\n\\n**Answer**: Play T3\"}"}
